d to be  moved from the place where they're made  up to be a part of the membrane so for  these secreted proteins and some  membrane proteins they're going to have  this um  this signal peptide that again it's like  it's a sequence that tells the cell  where this thing is supposed to go  but then once it gets up to the membrane  and it's either being secreted or  integrated into the membrane that signal  peptide needs to be cleaved off it needs  to be cut off and so that is necessary  for that polypeptide  to be able to fold  into its mature functional form that  signal peptide is going to be cut off  and it is the signal peptidase or the  space sometimes it's called that  that makes that cleavage happen  so these signal peptidases are found in  prokaryotes  and they're also found in the  endoplasmic reticulum and chloroplasts  and mitochondria  of various eukaryotes  so these are very very widely  distributed throughout all of the  domains of life very important to get  that signal peptide off so the rest of  the polypeptide can fold into its mature  functional form  now  all  known signal pet today says all the ones  that we know about are serene proteases  serine is one of the amino acids that  you may be familiar with  if you want to learn more about amino  acids and i do have a video  um on like a structure of amino acids  that you could check out if you want to  but the reason they're called serine  proteases you know there are protease  that means they cut a protein right uh  and they have a serine the anti  certain anti the amino acid serine is  part of their  active site the active site of their  enzyme if you want to know more about  active sites check out that video on  enzymes  now why  why am i excited about signal peptidases  i mean what they do is cool already on  its own but also a reason i think that  you should be excited about signal  peptidases as well is because they are  considered to be  a great  antibiotic  target  what does this mean  antibiotics target  different things in uh in bacterial  cells  okay so they will  target like the ribosome or they will  target the cell wall or they will target  uh the flagella some um because some  pathogens will have flagella  and  it's possible that the signal pep today  says  that bacteria use the bacteria require  we could potentially find an antibiotic  to inhibit those signal peptidases and  fight bacterial infections now at this  point at the point that i am filming  this video in early 2022  there are no approved antibiotics that  target signal peptidases  there are some that are like in trials  like some aerolomycins  but that's still something that we're  looking for  now why why is the signal peptidase of  bacteria such an attractive target for  antibiotics well first  it's essential  okay it is essential  to prokaryotes  so any  bacterial infection every single one of  those bacteria cells relies on a  functional signal peptidase  for to to um to sort of survive and to  replicate  so it's very essential now of course  it's essential for eukaryotes as well  right and humans are eukaryotes but  the signal peptidases and prokaryotes  are sufficiently  different  from their eukaryotic counterparts this  allows us to search for  selective toxicity and what that means  is  scientists are looking for a compound an  antibiotic  that would inhibit the activity of  prokaryotic  in this case we're talking about  bacteria right  of the bacterial signal peptidases but  without inhibiting the action of the  human eukaryotic signal peptidases so  finding an antibiotic that is  selectively toxic that will hurt the  bacterial  signal peptidase without affecting the  the human  signal peptidase  um and then also another reason it's  such a great antibiotic target is  because there's relatively easy access  to it remember that it's in the membrane  okay in the membrane  including the membrane of the bacteria  and its active site is actually out here  it's active site is on t